From 2c32f91e0c12633609f07e3136c25a75fb5452e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: aquarat <3090997+aquarat@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 4 Oct 2026 18:31:51 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 1/7] avcodec/v4l2_buffers: honour the OUTPUT format's
 bytesperline and height

Copying a frame into an OUTPUT buffer used the frame's own linesize as the
buffer's line stride and the format's height as the number of lines to
read from the frame. Drivers that align bytesperline beyond the width (a
720-wide NV12 picture with bytesperline 768) got a sheared picture, and
drivers that round the height up to whole macroblocks (1080 -> 1088) made
ffmpeg read eight lines past the end of the frame and crash.

Lay the buffer out as V4L2 defines it instead: plane 0 at bytesperline,
the chroma planes of a single-planar format at bytesperline * height
with the chroma line length derived from bytesperline, and every plane of
a multi-planar format at its own bytesperline. Read only the frame's
lines and columns, and repeat the last column and line into the padding
that the driver added, which the encoder codes and the stream crops.

The layout and copy live in v4l2_enc_utils.c, which does not touch the
device, with a unit test (fate-v4l2-enc-utils) covering 720x576 with a
768-byte stride, 1920x1080 in a 1920x1088 buffer, P010, I420 and NV12M.

Also map V4L2_PIX_FMT_P010 to AV_PIX_FMT_P010LE so that 10-bit input
can reach encoders that take it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01AGB1JqfDntxCaB5cKZV4vM
